 🕶️ It's Black Friday! Thanks for joining us for this doorbuster edition.

 Smart Brevity™ count: 1,359 words ... 5 mins. Thanks to Erica Pandey for orchestrating. Edited by Bill Kole. 🚀 Elon Musk pledged two SpaceX shares for every child in America ("one from the company and one from me personally"), doubling an offer he made just last month, in what's being called an " AI charm offensive ." 

 The $18 billion giveaway (worth $255 per kid ) roughly triples Michael Dell's once record-breaking Trump Accounts gift , Axios' Ben Berkowitz reports . In a later post on X, Musk said the program will continue: "I will be donating another share of SpaceX to all kids in America every year."

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Price of loyalty to Trump 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 JD Vance and Marco Rubio are the Republicans most likely to run to succeed President Trump. But the past few weeks have shown how saddled each would be by decisions he made against their advice.

 Why it matters: Trump's unpopularity cost Republicans the House and Senate. The Iran war and now the Jensen Huang imbroglio show how hard it'd be for Vance or Rubio to run on their own terms, Axios' Mike Zapler writes. The big picture: The problem isn't just that Vance and Rubio disagreed with Trump — it's that the two episodes cut to the core of their political identities.

 Vice President Vance has cast himself as a new-generation, anti-interventionist Republican who's wary of trying to solve the world's problems and has broken with GOP orthodoxy of unequivocal support for Israel. He made the case against going to war with Iran and blamed the war for the GOP's midterm shellacking. Secretary of State Rubio came up in the Senate as a foreign policy hawk and fierce critic of Russia and China, which sanctioned him in 2020. In May, aboard Air Force One en route to China, he warned Trump about Huang's relationship with a suspected Chinese spy. 🎤 What they're saying: In recent weeks, Vance and Rubio have had to confront the tradeoffs of serving under Trump in painfully public ways.

 Asked last week, with Trump standing beside him, whether the war was worth the GOP's election losses, Vance declined to say. "I think the president is the one who makes the decision, and the president has said he would do it again," Vance said . "And my view is that my goal is and my job is to support the president of the United States." When a reporter shouted a question to Rubio about Huang's reinstatement as Nvidia's president and CEO, the secretary of state walked away.

 Gerry Baker, Wall Street Journal editor at large, wrote this week in a column urging Rubio to leave the administration: "As it becomes evident that [Trump's] policy is shaming, corrupting and weakening America, [Rubio] has to keep fronting." Share this story . 

 
 
