 2. 🛒 America&#x27;s bargain hunt 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Americans are getting choosier about how they spend their money, with new signs of a nationwide bargain hunt.

 Why it matters: Consumer spending has remained resilient, helping power eye-popping economic growth. But a closer look at spending patterns suggests that households are trading down, hunting for deals and second-guessing purchases beyond the basics, Axios' Courtenay Brown reports. Zoom in: Kohl's CFO Jill Timm said earlier this week that higher-income consumers are trading down to its stores, while middle-income shoppers are choosing its cheaper in-house brands. 

 Discount grocer Grocery Outlet saw signs for the first time last quarter of shoppers trading down from pricier products, particularly premium beef, CEO Jason Potter said. Keep reading . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. 🌧️ Turkey Day turmoil 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Two men kayak down Hampton Boulevard in Norfolk, Va., on Thanksgiving morning as rain floods streets and strands drivers. Photo: Stephen M. Katz/The Virginian-Pilot via AP More than a million people nationwide were without power Thanksgiving, as a powerful nor'easter inundated communities from Virginia to New York and a winter storm blew through the Midwest.

 💥 By the numbers: As of 9 p.m. ET, PowerOutage.us reported more than 900,000 utility customers nationwide without power.

 That likely represents well more than a million people in the dark, Axios' Ben Berkowitz writes . (Depending on location, most utilities' unofficial rule of thumb is that one customer typically represents anywhere from two to four people.) King tides batter the oceanfront Marine Room restaurant in the La Jolla area of San Diego, Calif., on Thanksgiving. Photo: Sean M. Haffey/Getty Images For those not already at their destinations, travel quickly turned into a nightmare.

 FlightAware reported 2,800+ flights delayed or canceled; the Washington- and New York-area airports were worst affected. Get the latest . 

 
 
