 5. 💸 &quot;Tax the rich&quot; takes off 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Efforts to tax the rich are gaining steam at the state level.

 Why it matters: States are getting slammed with the prospect of less money coming in from the federal government and rising costs — and taxes on the highest earners, a relatively small group, are looking like a tidy solution to the problem, Axios' Emily Peck reports. 👀 State of play: In the midterms, voters in three states backed measures that would raise or preserve income taxes on higher earners.

 Washington rejected a measure that would've gotten rid of a new millionaire tax that imposes a 9.9% tax on income exceeding $1 million (for single filers) and $1.5 million for those filing jointly. (Washington has had zero state income tax until now.) Colorado approved one that replaces a flat income tax with one that raises taxes on the highest earners — giving most everyone else a cut. California voters made existing higher income tax rates permanent, though they rejected a measure that would have imposed a one-time tax of 5% on billionaires . Earlier this year, state legislators passed new millionaire income taxes in Maine (2% on income over $1 million) and Rhode Island (3% on income over $1 million). Hawai'i created a new higher tax bracket for those earning more than $500,000 (single filers) and $1 million (couples).

