 7. 🌐 Remembering Strobe Talbott 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President-elect Bill Clinton and TIME's Strobe Talbott talk privately as they walk along the beach on Hilton Head Island, S.C., on Jan. 2, 1993. Photo: James Finley/AP Strobe Talbott, a former TIME magazine star who later was deputy secretary of state under President Bill Clinton and then headed the Brookings Institution, died yesterday in his East Hampton, N.Y., home. He was 80. 

 Talbott was educated at Yale and Oxford, where he bunked with Clinton, AP reports . After Talbott interned for TIME in Moscow, the magazine asked him to help translate the leaked memoirs of Nikita Khrushchev, the former first secretary of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union, who was ousted in 1964.

 The assignment gave Talbott notoriety as a young man and a job at the magazine. Deputy Secretary of State Strobe Talbott accompanies Secretary of State Madeleine Albright at a U.S.-E.U. summit in London in 1998. Photo: David Hume Kennerly/Newsweek. Used by kind permission 📞 After 21 years at TIME — where he focused on Soviet-American relations, and held positions including Washington bureau chief and foreign-affairs columnist — he received a call from the newly elected Clinton.

 Talbott joined the State Department in 1994 and architected the Clinton administration's approach to the former Soviet bloc. N.Y. Times obit (gift link). 

 
 
